Ques9 : There is requiremwnt for the integration -- what all initial steps as an architect you do to for requirement gathering.
Ans 9:When I receive an integration requirement, I don't start with the technology or API. As an architect, I first understand the business process and then derive the technical solution.
First, I understand the business objective and scope — why the integration is needed, what business process it supports, and which systems are involved.
Second, I identify the source and target systems and establish the system of record. For each data domain, I want to know which system owns the data and whether the integration is one-way or bidirectional.
Third, I understand the data and relationships. I identify the objects, key fields, unique identifiers, parent-child relationships, and any referential-integrity requirements. I also understand whether historical information such as Created Date, Modified Date, and other business dates needs to be preserved.
Fourth, I define the integration behavior — what events trigger the integration, whether it needs to be real-time, near-real-time, or batch, and the expected data volume and frequency, including peak volumes.
Fifth, I gather the transformation and mapping requirements. I identify source-to-target field mappings, data-type conversions, picklist mappings, mandatory fields, default values, and business validation rules.
Then I focus on non-functional requirements such as performance, availability, security, authentication, data privacy, API limits, SLA, monitoring, and scalability.
I also define failure handling upfront — how errors are logged, retry mechanisms, duplicate handling or idempotency, dead-letter processing, alerting, and how failed transactions will be reprocessed.
Finally, I define reconciliation and operational requirements so that we can prove that the data sent by the source was successfully processed by Salesforce.

Once I have these requirements, I evaluate the appropriate integration pattern — for example REST/SOAP, Bulk API, Platform Events, CDC, or middleware such as MuleSoft — and then produce the solution architecture, data mapping, sequence diagrams, and implementation approach.
So my approach is essentially: understand the business → identify systems and ownership → understand data and events → define functional and non-functional requirements → define error and security requirements → and only then select the integration pattern and technology.”


Ques10. what are the limitation of Dynamic record form?
Ans 10: Mobile apps may not support Dynamic Forms, limiting the effectiveness of Salesforce applications for users who need to access them on the go
Slow page load times
If you have controlling and dependent fields in different field section components, the dependent field doesn’t update when you change the controlling field value.
Encrypted fields on a dynamic form don’t display correctly. The field label shows as “Encrypted Field” instead of the actual label.
Dynamic Forms are only available for objects that support Lightning Web Component (LWC) record pages.

Ques 11. how do you reduce the technical debt of any legacy salesforce org
Ams 11: run salesforce optimizer(retired) -- org check (app exchange) -- free
Static Code Analysis (PMD / Code Analyzer): Scans the Apex codebase for anti-patterns (e.g., SOQL/DML inside for loops, missing null checks, poor test classes without assertions, outdated API versions).
Dependency Analysis: Use tools like Salesforce Metadata Dependency API, HappySoup.io, or SonarQube to identify circular dependencies and orphaned metadata.
Scan for Data Skew (ownership skew where one user owns >10,000 records, or lookup skew where thousands of child records point to one parent), which causes row locks and slow SOQL queries.
Data Archiving:Implement an archival strategy (Big Objects, Salesforce Backup, or external data stores via AWS/Snowflake) to move historical, non-operational data out of standard objects.
Code Modernization: Update API versions, UI migration
